text:
Nice tool. Does it support local embedding providers such as qdrant <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;qdrant.tech&#x2F;" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;qdrant.tech&#x2F;</a> ? Why only support paid, cloud-based embeddings provider ?<p>ChatGPT says it could work without updating the code, but since README doesn&#x27;t advertise it, I was curious as to the reason behind this choice.<p>It says: &quot;Can Slopdex run without paid embedding APIs?<p>Potentially yes, without modifying its code.
[...] Slopdex supports a custom embeddingBaseUrl, allowing it to call a locally hosted, OpenAI-compatible embedding server.
For example, with a local model producing 768-dimensional vectors:
{
  &quot;provider&quot;: &quot;openai&quot;,
  &quot;model&quot;: &quot;your-local-embedding-model&quot;,
  &quot;dimensions&quot;: 768,
  &quot;symbolDimensions&quot;: 768,
  &quot;embeddingBaseUrl&quot;: &quot;http:&#x2F;&#x2F;localhost:1234&#x2F;v1&quot;
}<p>Its code still requires an OPENAI_API_KEY value, but a dummy key should work if your local server doesn&#x27;t enforce authentication.<p>The server must implement &#x2F;v1&#x2F;embeddings and accept Slopdex&#x27;s request format, including the dimensions parameter.
The relevant code is in vector_openai.rs.&quot;

while this points it out, i&#x27;ll show you what I had in place:<p>~&#x2F;.codex&#x2F;config.toml<p><pre><code>    [features]
    daemon_auto_start = false
</code></pre>
hell, I also have this redundancy configured in bash:<p><pre><code>    codex() {
      command codex --no-daemon &quot;${@:-.}&quot;
    }</code></pre>
